+Domain document for **PegaFilaWeb** — repository **frontend, backend and totem API** (Laravel monolith). Derived from product requirements. Core domain issuance is implemented via `App\Actions\IssueTicket` (ticket into an active queue) and deterministic call-next via `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (three call modes + 3-ticket fairness); queue lifecycle and per-queue employee access are implemented through `CreateQueue`, `UpdateQueue`, `SetQueueAccess` and `QueuePolicy`. HTTP, Livewire, and billing remain future work.
 
 ## MVP Scope
 
@@ -67,6 +67,37 @@ ## User Administration
 
 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue lifecycle
+
+Queues belong to one entity. Only managers (**owner/admin**) of that entity may create or update them; employees, members of other entities and non-members are rejected with `AuthorizationException` and nothing is written.
+
+- **Create** (`App\Actions\CreateQueue`): name is trimmed and must be 1–255 characters. The slug defaults to `Str::slug(name)` (for example `Atendimento Geral` → `atendimento-geral`); an explicit slug is also normalized with `Str::slug`. New queues are active.
+- **Update** (`App\Actions\UpdateQueue`): name, slug and active flag are independent and nullable — `null` leaves the current value unchanged.
+- **Slug uniqueness:** slugs are unique **within an entity**, so the same slug may exist in different entities. Duplicates (including a racing insert caught by the DB unique index) surface as `ValidationException`, never a raw `QueryException`.
+- **No hard delete:** a queue is retired by setting `is_active = false`. Its tickets and access grants are preserved, and issuing/calling on an inactive queue keeps failing with `InactiveQueueException`.
+
+### Per-queue employee access
+
+`queue_access` grants an `entity_membership` permissions on one queue. Today the only permission is `call` (`App\Enums\QueuePermission`). Managers set the full permission list with `App\Actions\SetQueueAccess`:
+
+- A non-empty list upserts the grant; an empty list revokes it.
+- Unknown permission strings or non-string entries raise `ValidationException` with nothing stored.
+- The membership and queue must belong to the same entity (otherwise `ValidationException`).
+- Non-managers — including an employee trying to grant themselves — receive `AuthorizationException`.
+- Successful grants/revokes write `queue.access.granted` / `queue.access.revoked` to the `audit` log channel with `actor_id`, `queue_id` and `entity_membership_id`. A revoke with no existing row is not logged.
+
+### Queue authorization policy
+
+`App\Policies\QueuePolicy` is the single source of truth used by both call paths:
+
+| Ability | Allowed |
+| ------- | ------- |
+| `create` (entity argument) | owner/admin of that entity |
+| `update`, `manageAccess` | owner/admin of the queue's entity |
+| `call` | owner/admin of the queue's entity **always**; an employee only with a `queue_access` row for **that** queue whose `permissions` array contains `call`; everyone else denied |
+
+`CallNextTicket` locks the queue, rejects inactive queues, then authorizes the caller for every call mode. `Ticket::call()` locks the queue, rejects inactive queues, authorizes the caller (even if the ticket was already called), and only then applies the “waiting tickets only” rule. Unauthorized calls leave tickets unchanged.
+
